      "content": "---\nid: medicare-supplements-medigap-and-senior-coverage\ntitle: \"Medicare Supplements (MediGap) & Senior Coverage (Official 5% Weight)\"\ntype: concept\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-10-25\nupdated: 2026-10-25\nsources:\n  - \"[[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\"\ntags:\n  - medigap\n  - senior-coverage\n  - medicare-supplements\n  - high-yield\n---\n\n# Medicare Supplements (MediGap) & Senior Coverage (Official 5% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nOriginal Medicare (Part A & Part B) pays a lot of hospital and doctor bills for seniors, but it leaves \"potholes\" called gaps\u2014like deductibles and 20% coinsurance.\n- **MediGap (Medicare Supplement)** is a private insurance patch that covers those potholes.\n- **The Core Rule**: MediGap **ONLY works with Original Medicare (Parts A & B)**.\n- If a senior decides to enroll in **Medicare Advantage (Part C)** instead, they **CANNOT use MediGap** to pay Medicare Advantage copays! In fact, it is illegal to sell a MediGap policy to someone who has Medicare Advantage unless they are switching back to Original Medicare!\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Rules & Distinctions\n- **Standardized Plans (Plans A through N)**:\n  - Lettered plans are identical across all insurance companies (e.g. Plan G from Company X has the exact same benefits as Plan G from Company Y; only prices differ).\n  - **Plan A is the Core Basic Benefit Plan**: Every insurer selling MediGap must offer Plan A.\n- **New York State Specific MediGap Rules (CRITICAL NY DISTINCTION)**:\n  - *Federal Rule*: 6-month open enrollment period upon turning 65 and enrolling in Part B.\n  - *New York State Rule*: **Continuous, year-round open enrollment and pure community rating**! Insurers selling MediGap in New York cannot deny coverage or charge higher premiums based on age, gender, or health status at ANY time of the year!\n- **Free-Look Period**: **30 Days** mandatory right of examination in New York with a 100% full refund.\n- **Pre-existing Condition Waiting Period in NY**: Maximum of **6 months**, which must be credited or waived if the individual had continuous prior creditable coverage.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recall Cue\n- **Medigap fills gaps in Original Medicare; Advantage replaces that route.**\n- **Plan A = Core Basic Benefits (Mandatory offer).**\n- **New York = Year-round continuous open enrollment & pure community rating.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: A client enrolls in a private Medicare Advantage plan (Part C) and asks an insurance agent to sell them a MediGap policy to pay the Medicare Advantage plan's specialist copayments. What is the most accurate response?\n> - A) MediGap is designed to supplement Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage.\n> - B) MediGap automatically pays all Medicare Advantage copayments.\n> - C) MediGap replaces Part D prescription drug coverage.\n> - D) Medicare Advantage and MediGap are interchangeable names for the same program.\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **A) MediGap is designed to supplement Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage.**\n> - **Explanation**: MediGap policies work exclusively with Original Medicare (Parts A & B) to fill cost-sharing gaps. It cannot be used to pay copayments under Medicare Advantage.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **B** is tempting if candidates believe MediGap pays copayments for any health plan.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- Comparative Matrix: [[medicare-vs-medicaid-vs-medigap]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]\n",
      "body": "# Medicare Supplements (MediGap) & Senior Coverage (Official 5% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nOriginal Medicare (Part A & Part B) pays a lot of hospital and doctor bills for seniors, but it leaves \"potholes\" called gaps\u2014like deductibles and 20% coinsurance.\n- **MediGap (Medicare Supplement)** is a private insurance patch that covers those potholes.\n- **The Core Rule**: MediGap **ONLY works with Original Medicare (Parts A & B)**.\n- If a senior decides to enroll in **Medicare Advantage (Part C)** instead, they **CANNOT use MediGap** to pay Medicare Advantage copays! In fact, it is illegal to sell a MediGap policy to someone who has Medicare Advantage unless they are switching back to Original Medicare!\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Rules & Distinctions\n- **Standardized Plans (Plans A through N)**:\n  - Lettered plans are identical across all insurance companies (e.g. Plan G from Company X has the exact same benefits as Plan G from Company Y; only prices differ).\n  - **Plan A is the Core Basic Benefit Plan**: Every insurer selling MediGap must offer Plan A.\n- **New York State Specific MediGap Rules (CRITICAL NY DISTINCTION)**:\n  - *Federal Rule*: 6-month open enrollment period upon turning 65 and enrolling in Part B.\n  - *New York State Rule*: **Continuous, year-round open enrollment and pure community rating**! Insurers selling MediGap in New York cannot deny coverage or charge higher premiums based on age, gender, or health status at ANY time of the year!\n- **Free-Look Period**: **30 Days** mandatory right of examination in New York with a 100% full refund.\n- **Pre-existing Condition Waiting Period in NY**: Maximum of **6 months**, which must be credited or waived if the individual had continuous prior creditable coverage.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recall Cue\n- **Medigap fills gaps in Original Medicare; Advantage replaces that route.**\n- **Plan A = Core Basic Benefits (Mandatory offer).**\n- **New York = Year-round continuous open enrollment & pure community rating.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: A client enrolls in a private Medicare Advantage plan (Part C) and asks an insurance agent to sell them a MediGap policy to pay the Medicare Advantage plan's specialist copayments. What is the most accurate response?\n> - A) MediGap is designed to supplement Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage.\n> - B) MediGap automatically pays all Medicare Advantage copayments.\n> - C) MediGap replaces Part D prescription drug coverage.\n> - D) Medicare Advantage and MediGap are interchangeable names for the same program.\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **A) MediGap is designed to supplement Original Medicare, not Medicare Advantage.**\n> - **Explanation**: MediGap policies work exclusively with Original Medicare (Parts A & B) to fill cost-sharing gaps. It cannot be used to pay copayments under Medicare Advantage.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **B** is tempting if candidates believe MediGap pays copayments for any health plan.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- Comparative Matrix: [[medicare-vs-medicaid-vs-medigap]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]",
