      "content": "---\nid: affordable-care-act-aca-and-essential-health-benefits\ntitle: \"Affordable Care Act (ACA), Metal Tiers & Essential Health Benefits\"\ntype: concept\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-10-25\nupdated: 2026-10-25\nsources:\n  - \"[[SRC-002-nys-dfs-psi-series-17-52-accident-health-outline]]\"\ntags:\n  - aca\n  - metal-tiers\n  - essential-health-benefits\n  - high-yield\n---\n\n# Affordable Care Act (ACA), Metal Tiers & Essential Health Benefits\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nThe **Affordable Care Act (ACA)** created fair national rules for health insurance:\n- **No Bullying (Pre-existing conditions)**: Before the ACA, if you had asthma or diabetes, insurance companies could say: \"We won't cover your asthma, or we will charge you $1,000 extra!\" Under the ACA, **they CANNOT reject you or charge you more for past sicknesses**!\n- **Kids Stay on Parent Plans until Age 26**: Even if you graduate college, get married, or move out, you can stay on your parents' plan until your 26th birthday. (In New York, an extra state law called the **\"Age 29 Law\"** allows young adults to stay on up to age 29 under certain conditions!).\n- **The Metal Tiers (Olympics of Health Plans)**: Plans are ranked like Olympic medals (Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum). A Bronze plan pays the least of your medical bills (you pay high deductibles, but low monthly prices), while a Platinum plan pays almost all your medical bills (very low deductibles, but high monthly prices).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 4 ACA Metal Tiers (Actuarial Value - HIGH FREQUENCY NUMBERS)\n\nActuarial value represents the percentage of average total healthcare costs the health plan pays vs. what the patient pays out of pocket:\n\n```text\n================================================================================\nACA METAL TIERS (Mnemonic: \"B-S-G-P: 60 - 70 - 80 - 90\")\n================================================================================\nTier         Plan Pays (Actuarial Value)    Patient Pays Out-of-Pocket\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nBRONZE                 60%                             40%\nSILVER                 70%*                            30%\nGOLD                   80%                             20%\nPLATINUM               90%                             10%\n================================================================================\n*Note: Silver plans are the benchmark plan used to calculate federal Cost-Sharing\nReductions (CSR) and Premium Tax Credits (PTC).\n```\n- **Catastrophic Plan**: Available only to individuals **under age 30**, or those with an approved economic hardship exemption. High deductible, low premium, covers 3 primary care visits per year and preventive care.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The 10 Essential Health Benefits (EHBs)\nUnder the ACA, all individual and small group plans must cover 10 mandatory categories of services without annual or lifetime dollar caps:\n1. Ambulatory patient services (outpatient care)\n2. Emergency services\n3. Hospitalization (inpatient care)\n4. Maternity and newborn care (cannot be excluded or charged extra!)\n5. Mental health and substance use disorder services (parity with medical/surgical)\n6. Prescription drugs\n7. Rehabilitative and habilitative services and devices\n8. Laboratory services\n9. Preventive and wellness services and chronic disease management ($0 copay!)\n10. Pediatric services, including oral (dental) and vision care for children\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key Federal & New York Underwriting Rules\n- **Community Rating in New York**: New York strictly enforces **Pure Community Rating** for individual and small group health insurance. Premiums CANNOT vary based on age, gender, or health status! (Federal ACA allows age rating up to 3:1 and tobacco rating, but New York state law forbids age and tobacco rating for individual/small group!).\n- **Guaranteed Issue & Guaranteed Renewable**: Coverage cannot be denied, and must be renewed regardless of health claims.\n- **Prohibition of Dollar Limits**: **Zero lifetime dollar limits** and **zero annual dollar limits** on Essential Health Benefits.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Exam Trap Question & Distractor Analysis\n\n> **Scenario**: Under the Affordable Care Act (ACA), what is the actuarial value of a Gold health insurance plan?\n> - A) 60%\n> - B) 70%\n> - C) 80%\n> - D) 90%\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **C) 80%**\n> - **Rationale**: Gold plans have an actuarial value of 80% (meaning the plan covers approximately 80% of average healthcare expenses, and the insured pays 20%).\n> - **Memory Hook**: **B**ronze = 60, **S**ilver = 70, **G**old = 80, **P**latinum = 90.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **D** is tempting because candidates think Gold is the top tier. In ACA, Platinum is the highest tier at 90%!\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cross-References\n- Medical Plans: [[medical-plans-cost-sharing-managed-care]]\n- Group Health: [[group-health-insurance-cobra-ny-continuation]]\n- Cram Sheet: [[ny-17-52-high-yield-exam-cram-sheet]]\n",
