      "content": "---\nid: government-insurance-plans\ntitle: \"Government Insurance Plans (Official 9% Weight)\"\ntype: concept\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-10-25\nupdated: 2026-10-25\nsources:\n  - \"[[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\"\ntags:\n  - government-plans\n  - medicare\n  - medicaid\n  - workers-comp\n  - ny-dbl\n  - high-yield\n---\n\n# Government Insurance Plans (Official 9% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nGovernment programs are public safety nets created by law for specific groups:\n- **Workers' Compensation**: For injuries that happen **ON the job** at work. Pays 100% of doctor/hospital bills and replaces part of your wages.\n- **New York DBL**: For sickness or injuries that happen **OFF the job** (like breaking a leg on a weekend trip). Pays a small weekly cash check, but **$0 for hospital bills**.\n- **Social Security Disability (SSDI)**: Severe federal program. You must have a disability so terrible that you cannot do any work at all, expected to last at least **12 months** or result in death, with a strict **5-month waiting period**.\n- **Medicare**: Federal health plan for folks age **65 and older**, or younger people with long-term disabilities (after 24 months of SSDI) or kidney failure (ESRD).\n- **Medicaid**: State and federal joint program for people with **limited income and low financial assets**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Program Rules & Distinctions\n- **Medicare Structure**:\n  - *Part A (Hospital)*: Inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility (SNF under defined conditions), hospice, home health.\n  - *Part B (Medical)*: Physician visits, outpatient hospital services, durable medical equipment. (Requires voluntary monthly premium; 80/20 coinsurance).\n  - *Part C (Medicare Advantage)*: Private managed care alternative to Original Medicare.\n  - *Part D (Prescriptions)*: Standalone prescription drug coverage through private approved plans.\n  - *CRITICAL RULE*: **Medicare DOES NOT cover ongoing custodial long-term nursing care!**\n- **Medicaid (Title XIX)**:\n  - Jointly funded by federal and state governments; administered in NY by the Department of Health.\n  - Means-tested (income and asset criteria apply). Covers hospital, doctor, prescription drugs, and **custodial nursing home care**. Subject to 60-month look-back on asset transfers.\n- **Child Health Plus (New York)**:\n  - New York's state health insurance plan for children under age 19; sliding scale premiums based on family income.\n- **Workers' Comp vs. New York DBL (CRITICAL EXAM NUMBERS)**:\n  - *Workers' Comp*: Work-related; pays medical bills + disability income.\n  - *NY DBL (Statutory Baseline)*: Non-work related; **50% of average weekly wage up to statutory cap of $170/week**, for up to **26 weeks**, with a **7-day waiting period** (begins on 8th day). Cash wage replacement only; no medical bill coverage!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recall Cue\n- **A = Admission (Hospital); B = Both doctors and outpatient; C = Choice of private plan; D = Drugs.**\n- **NY DBL = Non-work, $170 cap, 26 weeks, 7-day wait, Cash only.**\n- **Medicare \u2260 Ongoing Custodial Nursing Care.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: Which healthcare program is designed primarily for eligible individuals with limited income and assets and is jointly funded by federal and state governments?\n> - A) Medicare\n> - B) Medicaid\n> - C) Medigap\n> - D) Medicare Advantage\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **B) Medicaid**\n> - **Explanation**: Medicaid is the joint federal-state means-tested program administered by states under federal guidelines. Medicare is a purely federal social insurance program based on age or disability, not income.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **A (Medicare)** is tempting if candidates mix up Medicare (age/disability) and Medicaid (income/needs).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- NY DBL Deep Dive: [[new-york-disability-benefits-law-dbl]]\n- Senior Coverage: [[medicare-vs-medicaid-vs-medigap]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]\n",
      "body": "# Government Insurance Plans (Official 9% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nGovernment programs are public safety nets created by law for specific groups:\n- **Workers' Compensation**: For injuries that happen **ON the job** at work. Pays 100% of doctor/hospital bills and replaces part of your wages.\n- **New York DBL**: For sickness or injuries that happen **OFF the job** (like breaking a leg on a weekend trip). Pays a small weekly cash check, but **$0 for hospital bills**.\n- **Social Security Disability (SSDI)**: Severe federal program. You must have a disability so terrible that you cannot do any work at all, expected to last at least **12 months** or result in death, with a strict **5-month waiting period**.\n- **Medicare**: Federal health plan for folks age **65 and older**, or younger people with long-term disabilities (after 24 months of SSDI) or kidney failure (ESRD).\n- **Medicaid**: State and federal joint program for people with **limited income and low financial assets**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Program Rules & Distinctions\n- **Medicare Structure**:\n  - *Part A (Hospital)*: Inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility (SNF under defined conditions), hospice, home health.\n  - *Part B (Medical)*: Physician visits, outpatient hospital services, durable medical equipment. (Requires voluntary monthly premium; 80/20 coinsurance).\n  - *Part C (Medicare Advantage)*: Private managed care alternative to Original Medicare.\n  - *Part D (Prescriptions)*: Standalone prescription drug coverage through private approved plans.\n  - *CRITICAL RULE*: **Medicare DOES NOT cover ongoing custodial long-term nursing care!**\n- **Medicaid (Title XIX)**:\n  - Jointly funded by federal and state governments; administered in NY by the Department of Health.\n  - Means-tested (income and asset criteria apply). Covers hospital, doctor, prescription drugs, and **custodial nursing home care**. Subject to 60-month look-back on asset transfers.\n- **Child Health Plus (New York)**:\n  - New York's state health insurance plan for children under age 19; sliding scale premiums based on family income.\n- **Workers' Comp vs. New York DBL (CRITICAL EXAM NUMBERS)**:\n  - *Workers' Comp*: Work-related; pays medical bills + disability income.\n  - *NY DBL (Statutory Baseline)*: Non-work related; **50% of average weekly wage up to statutory cap of $170/week**, for up to **26 weeks**, with a **7-day waiting period** (begins on 8th day). Cash wage replacement only; no medical bill coverage!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recall Cue\n- **A = Admission (Hospital); B = Both doctors and outpatient; C = Choice of private plan; D = Drugs.**\n- **NY DBL = Non-work, $170 cap, 26 weeks, 7-day wait, Cash only.**\n- **Medicare \u2260 Ongoing Custodial Nursing Care.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: Which healthcare program is designed primarily for eligible individuals with limited income and assets and is jointly funded by federal and state governments?\n> - A) Medicare\n> - B) Medicaid\n> - C) Medigap\n> - D) Medicare Advantage\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **B) Medicaid**\n> - **Explanation**: Medicaid is the joint federal-state means-tested program administered by states under federal guidelines. Medicare is a purely federal social insurance program based on age or disability, not income.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **A (Medicare)** is tempting if candidates mix up Medicare (age/disability) and Medicaid (income/needs).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- NY DBL Deep Dive: [[new-york-disability-benefits-law-dbl]]\n- Senior Coverage: [[medicare-vs-medicaid-vs-medigap]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]",
