      "content": "---\nid: individual-health-policy-provisions-and-renewability\ntitle: \"Individual Health Policy Provisions & Renewability (Official 10% Weight)\"\ntype: concept\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-10-25\nupdated: 2026-10-25\nsources:\n  - \"[[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\"\ntags:\n  - policy-provisions\n  - renewability\n  - mandatory-provisions\n  - high-yield\n---\n\n# Individual Health Policy Provisions & Renewability (Official 10% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nPolicy provisions are the contract rules that explain who has rights and who has duties.\n- **Renewability Clauses (The Job Security of Your Policy)**:\n  - *Noncancelable (Ironclad)*: The company cannot cancel your policy, and they **cannot raise your price ever**!\n  - *Guaranteed Renewable (Very Good)*: The company cannot cancel your policy as long as you pay premiums, but **they CAN raise your price if they raise it for everyone in your rating class** statewide.\n  - *Conditionally Renewable (Conditional)*: The company can stop renewing only if a specific event mentioned in the policy happens (e.g. you reach age 65 or lose your job).\n  - *Optionally Renewable (Weak)*: The company can decide every year whether they feel like keeping you as a customer.\n  - *Cancelable (Weakest)*: The company can drop you anytime with written notice.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Mandatory Provisions (Uniform NAIC & NYIL \u00a7 3216)\n- **Entire Contract**: Policy + attached application + endorsements. Only an executive officer of the insurer can change terms; the agent has **zero authority** to waive or alter provisions.\n- **Time Limit on Certain Defenses**: **2 Years** (analogous to life incontestability). After 2 years, no misstatements (except fraud) can void coverage or deny claims.\n- **Grace Periods**: **7 days** (weekly premium), **10 days** (monthly premium), **31 days** (all other premium modes).\n- **Reinstatement**: Reinstatement is automatic on the **45th day** if insurer takes no action. Accidents covered immediately; **sickness covered after a 10-day probationary period**.\n- **Claims Countdown (20-15-90-60-3)**:\n  - *Notice of Claim*: 20 days.\n  - *Claim Forms*: 15 days.\n  - *Proof of Loss*: 90 days.\n  - *Time of Payment*: Immediately upon proof.\n  - *Legal Actions*: Minimum wait of 60 days after proof of loss before filing suit; maximum 3 years to initiate legal action.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Coordination of Benefits (COB)\n- Coordinates payments when an individual is covered under more than one group plan.\n- Designed to establish primary vs. secondary payer order and **prevent double recovery (profiting from a loss)**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Recall Cue\n- **\"Noncancelable locks cancellation and rate; guaranteed renewable locks renewal.\"**\n- **Grace periods = 7 - 10 - 31.**\n- **Reinstatement = 10-day sickness wait; accidents immediate.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: An insurer may raise a guaranteed-renewable policy's premium for all policyholders in a permitted class, but cannot refuse to renew an individual insured just because that person became ill. Which provision is this?\n> - A) Noncancelable\n> - B) Guaranteed renewable\n> - C) Optionally renewable\n> - D) Nonrenewable\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **B) Guaranteed renewable**\n> - **Explanation**: Guaranteed renewable contracts guarantee the right of the insured to renew the policy up to a specified age (often 65), but reserve the insurer's right to adjust premium rates on a class-wide basis.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **A (Noncancelable)** is tempting, but noncancelable policies forbid ANY premium increases.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- Detailed Provisions: [[mandatory-and-optional-health-provisions]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]\n",
      "body": "# Individual Health Policy Provisions & Renewability (Official 10% Weight)\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nPolicy provisions are the contract rules that explain who has rights and who has duties.\n- **Renewability Clauses (The Job Security of Your Policy)**:\n  - *Noncancelable (Ironclad)*: The company cannot cancel your policy, and they **cannot raise your price ever**!\n  - *Guaranteed Renewable (Very Good)*: The company cannot cancel your policy as long as you pay premiums, but **they CAN raise your price if they raise it for everyone in your rating class** statewide.\n  - *Conditionally Renewable (Conditional)*: The company can stop renewing only if a specific event mentioned in the policy happens (e.g. you reach age 65 or lose your job).\n  - *Optionally Renewable (Weak)*: The company can decide every year whether they feel like keeping you as a customer.\n  - *Cancelable (Weakest)*: The company can drop you anytime with written notice.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Mandatory Provisions (Uniform NAIC & NYIL \u00a7 3216)\n- **Entire Contract**: Policy + attached application + endorsements. Only an executive officer of the insurer can change terms; the agent has **zero authority** to waive or alter provisions.\n- **Time Limit on Certain Defenses**: **2 Years** (analogous to life incontestability). After 2 years, no misstatements (except fraud) can void coverage or deny claims.\n- **Grace Periods**: **7 days** (weekly premium), **10 days** (monthly premium), **31 days** (all other premium modes).\n- **Reinstatement**: Reinstatement is automatic on the **45th day** if insurer takes no action. Accidents covered immediately; **sickness covered after a 10-day probationary period**.\n- **Claims Countdown (20-15-90-60-3)**:\n  - *Notice of Claim*: 20 days.\n  - *Claim Forms*: 15 days.\n  - *Proof of Loss*: 90 days.\n  - *Time of Payment*: Immediately upon proof.\n  - *Legal Actions*: Minimum wait of 60 days after proof of loss before filing suit; maximum 3 years to initiate legal action.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Coordination of Benefits (COB)\n- Coordinates payments when an individual is covered under more than one group plan.\n- Designed to establish primary vs. secondary payer order and **prevent double recovery (profiting from a loss)**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Recall Cue\n- **\"Noncancelable locks cancellation and rate; guaranteed renewable locks renewal.\"**\n- **Grace periods = 7 - 10 - 31.**\n- **Reinstatement = 10-day sickness wait; accidents immediate.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. Tricky Exam Scenario & Trap Analysis\n> **Question**: An insurer may raise a guaranteed-renewable policy's premium for all policyholders in a permitted class, but cannot refuse to renew an individual insured just because that person became ill. Which provision is this?\n> - A) Noncancelable\n> - B) Guaranteed renewable\n> - C) Optionally renewable\n> - D) Nonrenewable\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **B) Guaranteed renewable**\n> - **Explanation**: Guaranteed renewable contracts guarantee the right of the insured to renew the policy up to a specified age (often 65), but reserve the insurer's right to adjust premium rates on a class-wide basis.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **A (Noncancelable)** is tempting, but noncancelable policies forbid ANY premium increases.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Cross-References\n- Primary Outline Source: [[SRC-003-psi-current-bulletin-ny-17-52-ah-study-guide]]\n- Detailed Provisions: [[mandatory-and-optional-health-provisions]]\n- Master Curriculum: [[ny-17-52-official-psi-study-guide-master]]",
