      "content": "---\nid: medical-plans-cost-sharing-managed-care\ntitle: \"Medical Plans, Cost Sharing & Managed Care (HMO, PPO, EPO, POS, Indemnity)\"\ntype: concept\nstatus: active\ncreated: 2026-10-25\nupdated: 2026-10-25\nsources:\n  - \"[[SRC-002-nys-dfs-psi-series-17-52-accident-health-outline]]\"\ntags:\n  - managed-care\n  - cost-sharing\n  - hmo-ppo-epo\n  - high-yield\n---\n\n# Medical Plans, Cost Sharing & Managed Care\n\n## 1. Plain-English Concept (For a 10-Year-Old)\nImagine a playground with different rules for seeing doctors:\n- **Traditional Indemnity (Fee-for-Service)**: You can visit ANY doctor you want in the whole city. You pay the doctor cash, bring the receipt back to your insurance company, and they pay you back a big part of it.\n- **HMO (Health Maintenance Organization)**: You get a \"Team Captain\" doctor called a **Primary Care Physician (PCP) / Gatekeeper**. If your ear hurts, you CANNOT just go see an ear specialist. You MUST ask your PCP first for a permission slip (called a **referral**). If you go outside the HMO playground (except for life-or-death emergencies), the HMO pays **zero dollars**!\n- **PPO (Preferred Provider Organization)**: You have a club list of \"favorite\" in-network doctors. You can go outside the club list, but you have to pay much more out of your own piggy bank. Good news: **NO gatekeeper PCP and NO referrals needed**!\n- **EPO (Exclusive Provider Organization)**: Like an HMO because there is **NO out-of-network coverage**, but like a PPO because you **don't need a gatekeeper referral** to see an in-network specialist.\n- **POS (Point of Service)**: A hybrid \"pick-at-the-moment-you-need-care\" plan. You have an HMO gatekeeper, but at the \"point of service,\" you can choose to go out of network if you are willing to pay higher deductibles and coinsurance.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Cost-Sharing Mechanics (Exam Rules & Calculations)\n\n```text\nTotal Medical Bill\n  \u2502\n  \u251c\u2500\u25ba [1] Insured pays DEDUCTIBLE first out-of-pocket\n  \u2502\n  \u251c\u2500\u25ba [2] Remaining balance split: COINSURANCE (e.g., 80% Insurer / 20% Insured)\n  \u2502\n  \u2514\u2500\u25ba [3] Once Insured's total payments hit STOP-LOSS (Out-of-Pocket Maximum):\n            Insurer pays 100% of all covered expenses for the rest of the year!\n```\n\n- **Deductible**: Stated dollar amount the insured must pay before policy benefits begin.\n- **Copayment (Copay)**: Fixed flat dollar fee (e.g., $25 per office visit) paid at the time of service.\n- **Coinsurance**: Percentage sharing of expenses after the deductible (commonly 80/20 or 70/30).\n- **Stop-Loss Provision (Out-of-Pocket Limit)**: The maximum dollar cap an insured pays in deductible + coinsurance during a calendar year. Once reached, insurer pays **100%**.\n\n---\n\n## 3. High-Yield Managed Care Comparison Matrix\n\n| Feature | Indemnity | HMO | PPO | EPO | POS |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Provider Network** | Any provider | Strictly In-Network | In & Out-of-Network | Strictly In-Network | In & Out-of-Network |\n| **Primary Care Physician (PCP)** | None | **Mandatory Gatekeeper** | None | None | **Mandatory Gatekeeper** |\n| **Specialist Referral** | Not needed | **Mandatory** | Not needed | Not needed | **Mandatory** |\n| **Out-of-Network Coverage** | Yes (Anywhere) | **NO** (Emergency only) | Yes (Higher cost) | **NO** (Emergency only) | Yes (Higher cost) |\n| **Payment Mechanism** | Fee-for-Service reimbursement | **Capitation** (Fixed monthly fee per head) | Negotiated Discounted Fee-for-Service | Negotiated Discounted Fee-for-Service | Mixed (Capitation & Fee) |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Limited Policies (High Frequency Definitions)\n- **Hospital Indemnity (Hospital Confinement / Fixed Rate)**: Pays a flat daily cash benefit (e.g., $100/day) directly to the insured for each day hospitalized, regardless of actual medical bills.\n- **Dread Disease (Critical Illness / Specified Disease)**: Covers only one specifically named disease (e.g., Cancer, Heart Attack).\n- **Accidental Death & Dismemberment (AD&D)**: Principal sum (capital amount) paid for death or double dismemberment (loss of 2 limbs or total sight); Capital sum (50%) paid for single dismemberment (loss of 1 limb or 1 eye).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Memory Hooks & Mnemonics\n- **HMO = Home/Mini playground, Must have gatekeeper, Outside = Zero.**\n- **PPO = Preferred Provider, Pick any specialist, Pay more Out-of-network.**\n- **EPO = Exclusive Playground Only (No Out-of-network, but No gatekeeper).**\n- **AD&D sums = Principal for Passing away (Death); Capital for Chopping off a limb.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Exam Trap Question & Distractor Analysis\n\n> **Scenario**: An insured covered under an HMO wakes up with knee pain. Wanting immediate expert advice, the insured goes directly to an in-network orthopedic surgeon without visiting their Primary Care Physician (PCP). The HMO denies the claim. Which provision justifies the denial?\n> - A) The coinsurance requirement was not satisfied.\n> - B) The gatekeeper / referral system was bypassed.\n> - C) The specialist was out-of-network.\n> - D) Orthopedic care is an excluded benefit under HMO law.\n>\n> **Correct Answer**: **B) The gatekeeper / referral system was bypassed.**\n> - **Rationale**: In an HMO, members MUST obtain a referral from their primary care physician (gatekeeper) before seeing a specialist, even if the specialist is in-network.\n> - **Tempting Wrong Answer**: **C** is tempting because people think claims are only denied when out-of-network. In HMOs, in-network specialist claims are denied if the gatekeeper is skipped!\n\n---\n\n## 7. Cross-References\n- Outline Source: [[SRC-002-nys-dfs-psi-series-17-52-accident-health-outline]]\n- Policy Provisions: [[mandatory-and-optional-health-provisions]]\n- ACA Standards: [[affordable-care-act-aca-and-essential-health-benefits]]\n",
